WEBVTT

00:00:02.183 --> 00:00:04.773
First of all, if you're under 2000 rating,

00:00:04.773 --> 00:00:07.943
improving tactics is the most important thing,
by far.

00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.583
Position and strategy don't matter
when somebody blunders even once.

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:15.353
But there are two types of tactics,
and I'll tell you which one you should train.

00:00:15.843 --> 00:00:18.623
There are calculations, which is
your ability to calculate

00:00:18.623 --> 00:00:20.443
and visualize a complicated move.

00:00:20.863 --> 00:00:22.153
This is your active thinking.

00:00:22.643 --> 00:00:25.453
But let's be real.
If you look at your last ten losses,

00:00:25.653 --> 00:00:27.673
you didn't lose due to
a complicated attack.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.343
You lost because of a simple blunder

00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:32.263
or because you missed
a simple tactic to win a piece.

00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:35.963
What you need is a training method
that improves your pattern recognition.

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:40.923
That's your brain's ability to instantly
recognize a pattern without having to calculate first.

00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:44.113
Look, you don't have to calculate
to see this checkmate.

00:00:44.913 --> 00:00:46.323
That's pattern recognition.

00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:49.163
Some people try to improve
with tactical puzzles.

00:00:49.643 --> 00:00:53.083
But they're mostly improving their
calculations with difficult tactics.

00:00:53.523 --> 00:00:55.453
They often end up
with a high puzzle rating.

00:00:55.793 --> 00:00:57.733
But their real chess rating
doesn't improve.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:01.753
Because in a real game, you don't
carefully calculate every move like a puzzle.

00:01:02.003 --> 00:01:04.293
You lack simple pattern recognition

00:01:04.293 --> 00:01:06.813
to spot every single blunder
and simple tactic on both sides.

00:01:07.213 --> 00:01:10.893
Even your higher calculations rely
on recognizing the basic pattern.

00:01:11.353 --> 00:01:13.753
So, you have been training
your calculations,

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:16.023
but you need to train
your pattern recognition.

00:01:16.483 --> 00:01:18.933
Playing many, many chess games
is one way to do that.

00:01:19.393 --> 00:01:22.423
But you probably don't want to play
for an hour every day for years.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.910
Especially if you blunder a piece
or miss a tactic even once

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.100
in five games, this is made for you.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.960
If you play puzzles and feel like
your high puzzle rating

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.660
isn't converting into a good chess rating,

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:35.250
this is perfect for you.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.200
If you have a feeling that you could avoid
most mistakes if you paid attention,

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.810
but you get lazy while playing
and blunder by random,

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.020
then pattern recognition training
will be a game changer for you.
